1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
of the indignity of Crowley, the victim of a crime.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:07,000
All right, so that's one answer that a lot of people give.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,800
Say, well, then how can we live in this neighborhood

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:12,600
where the rent is $100 less than that neighborhood

5
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,600
where the crime is, even though the crime here is 5% higher?

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:19,000
Well, I mean, I can't afford to live in a place $100 more expensive.

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,100
Really? But you have cable television, you know, you have a phone,

8
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:23,000
that comes out tomorrow.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,400
Well, you can't live without cable television and a phone.

10
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:28,600
So, in other words, you can put a price on it.

11
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:33,000
It's worth cable TV plus a phone to endure this risk.

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,400
Okay, so there is a literature trying to use actual market behavior

13
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:40,000
to get a better measure of how much people value things.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,200
Although, of course, that only shows you what the marginal person thinks.

15
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:48,800
So, it's possible that there is one really extreme weirdo

16
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:50,800
who is not the marginal person at all,

17
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,200
who really does put some enormous value on the things that they claim.

18
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,600
So, maybe there is a person out there who really,

19
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:01,200
I would rather die than see one acre of Alaska be blackened by soot.

20
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:04,000
All right, maybe.

21
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
So, when you really think about it,

22
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,200
to do perfectly accurate cost-benefit analysis,

23
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:13,000
you would need to have a superpower.

24
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,400
You would have to have migrating.

25
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:16,800
You would have to have telepathy to do it perfectly.

26
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:19,400
Of course, that does not mean that you can't do a,

27
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:23,400
that you can't try to make a reasonable guess at it.

28
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:25,600
In the same way that you're trying to figure out

29
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:27,400
what would make this child happier,

30
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:31,400
a party at Puppet Up or a party at home.

31
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:35,000
So, you have a lot, you could ask the child.

32
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,000
That's one thing you could do,

33
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,600
but maybe the child doesn't know what's going on,

34
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:41,200
or maybe the child lies for his own nefarious reasons.

35
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,400
And then you could try to go and figure,

36
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:45,200
and then you could take other signs.

37
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:47,200
So, you might go, let's go and look at,

38
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:49,800
like, what was his face, you might look at his face.

39
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:51,200
You might go and see,

40
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,000
and look at how hard he tries to get one result versus another.

41
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,800
There's a lot of things that you might do

42
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:57,800
to try to figure out whether someone's lying.

43
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:00,000
And in fact, of course, we have a whole list of tools

44
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:01,200
that we use to try to figure out

45
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:03,400
whether someone is being honest with us.

46
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:07,200
Right, which, you know, some of them don't work that well.

47
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:08,800
Actually, there's a whole literature on that, too.

48
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:12,200
Like, lie detection, how good are we at it?

49
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:14,000
And then, of course, once in a while,

50
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,400
we get these golden opportunities

51
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:17,200
to contrast what people say with what they do.

52
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:18,600
Like, someone who says,

53
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:21,400
I will leave the country if Trump gets elected.

54
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:23,400
And then we can see whether they really leave.

55
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:25,800
So, those people, at least,

56
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:27,600
were overstating how much they cared.

57
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:29,200
They might have changed their mind.

58
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:32,200
I realize that as much as I would like to leave the country,

59
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:34,000
the country needs me.

60
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,600
So, I cannot do what I wanted to do.

61
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,200
I must do what the country now requires of me.

62
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:42,600
So, there's always these stories,

63
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:45,200
but nevertheless, there are ways

64
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:47,400
you might try to do quality empirical work

65
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:51,200
by sorting through what seems more or less like to be true.

66
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:52,200
Yes?

67
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:54,400
Is this Hicks, the same Hicks of the Slutsky decomposition?

68
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:55,600
Yep, sure is.

69
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:58,000
Yep, he did a bunch of things.

70
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,200
Yes, Calver, like, there's one or two other things

71
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:01,400
he's kind of known for,

72
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:05,400
but nothing as famous as the Hicks-Slutsky decomposition,

73
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:08,200
which, of course, is discussed very heatedly in the media

74
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:11,000
on a daily basis.

75
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,400
All right, now, Calver, oh, yes, by the way,

76
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:14,800
so one thing that I did not tell you

77
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:18,200
on the bureaucratic issue of this class

78
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:20,800
is you are entitled to two things in this class.

79
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:24,000
First of all, you are entitled to a 10-minute break,

80
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,200
not less, but not more, either.

81
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:27,800
So, the break will be the middle class,

82
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,200
normally at 8.35 every day, from 8.35 to 8.45.

83
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:32,800
So, you are entitled to that.

84
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:34,400
If I appear to not be giving that to you,

85
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:35,800
you have every right to interrupt me

86
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:38,000
and say, stop on break time.

87
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,600
And also, I will strive to land on time down to the minute

88
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:44,200
because while, as graduate students,

89
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:46,200
you are expected to be willing to do economics

90
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:50,000
until 10 o'clock, regardless of how much you are suffering,

91
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,600
you are not expected to do it at 10.01.

92
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:55,800
So, that is why we will go to the end of every class,

93
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:58,000
no matter how tired we may seem,

94
