but I ask all that of you, but no more.

95
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:05,800
And if I am at 10.01, totally appropriate to go

96
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:09,200
and call time on me to signal that you have dealt with it

97
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:11,000
and there's enough, because there's also some people

98
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:12,200
who have to catch buses and things,

99
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:15,800
so it's even worse than just discourteous to go over

100
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:17,000
the allotted time.

101
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:17,800
Good question.

102
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:18,200
Yes.

103
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:21,600
Is it always going exactly to time inefficient?

104
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:23,000
Let's see.

105
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,200
Possibly, probably a little bit.

106
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:27,800
Good question.

107
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:29,800
Homework problem now.

108
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:30,400
All right.

109
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:31,900
Now, counter-exhibitionism naturally

110
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:33,600
gives rise to another concept that you're

111
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:38,200
likely to have heard about more, which is the deadweight cost.

112
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:40,400
So, how many people have heard about deadweight cost

113
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:42,600
at some point in their economic education?

114
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:45,400
All right, so if the value of social resources

115
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:47,600
did not maximize, deadweight costs exist.

116
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:49,000
Deadweight costs are by definition

117
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,400
the difference between the actual dollar value

118
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:54,000
of social resources and the potential value.

119
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,600
So, take the maximum value, subtract the actual,

120
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:00,200
that difference is equal to the deadweight cost.

121
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:03,000
So, any time you saw a diagram of monopoly,

122
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,600
something like this, marginal cost, demand,

123
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:11,800
marginal revenue, any time you saw that,

124
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:13,600
that deadweight loss triangle was

125
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:17,300
the difference between maximizing the dollar value

126
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:21,200
of social resources, which would occur here,

127
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:24,200
and the actual dollar value of social resource, which is that.

128
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:26,600
And the difference is that deadweight loss triangle.

129
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:28,400
All right, so that's what deadweight losses

130
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:29,200
are by definition.

131
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:33,900
It is the difference between reality and potentiality

132
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:37,100
in terms of dollar value of social resources.

133
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:41,600
All right, now, why is this so important or interesting?

134
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:44,000
So, here is my favorite way of thinking about it.

135
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,400
Suppose that someone comes to you and says,

136
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:49,600
tell us what economists know that normal people don't.

137
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:51,200
All right, now, hopefully the following

138
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:52,800
will not be your answer.

139
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:55,200
Here's what economists know that no one else does.

140
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:58,400
It's possible to take resources from one person

141
00:05:58,400 --> 00:05:59,900
and give them to another.

142
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:03,200
All right, so that is the case of transferring resources.

143
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:05,500
And it's something you don't need any economics in order

144
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:06,100
to understand.

145
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:09,300
Everybody knows about transferring resources.

146
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:12,100
Something that is a better answer to this question,

147
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:15,100
that resources can be destroyed, leaving no one better off.

148
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:16,800
And those resources that are destroyed,

149
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:19,700
leaving no one better off, are the deadweight costs.

150
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:21,800
All right, so here's an example that I actually

151
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:24,300
taught to Peter Leeson when he was in your shoes,

152
00:06:24,300 --> 00:06:26,100
or in your seat, in a different room.

153
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:29,300
But, so, here's an example that I actually

154
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:31,500
taught to Peter Leeson when he was in your shoes.

155
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:33,500
So, you know, Peter Leeson has his book on piracy.

156
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:35,300
So I don't think my book about this lecture

157
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:36,900
inspired his book on piracy.

158
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:38,900
But I think he had other interests in piracy.

159
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:40,500
But anyway, piracy.

160
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:43,900
So if you were to go and just give a really simple minded

161
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:46,100
theory of piracy, it would just be

162
00:06:46,100 --> 00:06:50,100
pirates transfer resources from victims to themselves.

163
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:52,300
That's what piracy is.

164
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:54,500
But there are also deadweight costs of piracy,

165
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:56,500
which when you really start listing them,

166
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:59,500
they overshadow whatever it is the pirates are transferring.

167
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,900
So just for starters, if there's ever

168
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:04,300
any treasure that gets destroyed during the fight,

169
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:05,700
if the pirates shoot their cannon

170
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:08,500
and it hits a treasure chest and blows it out of the ship

171
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:11,700
and it's destroyed, then who got that treasure chest?

172
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:12,500
Davy Jones.

173
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:14,700
Yes, Davy Jones is lost.

174
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:18,700
And which is slang for nobody, right?

175
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:21,700
So the victims lost it, but the pirates did not gain it.

176
00:07:21,700 --> 00:07:23,200
And then you get damage to the ships.

177
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:24,800
Who gained the damage?

178
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:27,000
Who gained the hole in the hull?

179
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,700
No one gained the hole in the hull.

180
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:29,700
It's just destroyed.

181
00:07:29,700 --> 00:07:31,100
It's lost to mankind.

182
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:34,800
Or how about some people die in the fight, pirates and victims.

183
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:36,300
Who gained those deaths?

184
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:39,300
Who is now the proud owner of those souls?

185
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:39,800
No one.

186
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:42,000
They're just lost.

187
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,200
And then if you start going and thinking about incentives,

188
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:47,700
then you realize, well, so what might

189
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:52,000
victims do if they know that the Caribbean is a center of piracy?

190
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,900
How might potential victims revise their behavior?

191
00:07:54,900 --> 00:07:55,700
Circumvent.

192
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:58,100
You'd go around the Caribbean, then you

193
00:07:58,100 --> 00:08:00,700
have deadweight costs of the extra transportation costs

194
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:02,700
that are spent circumventing the piracy.

195
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:04,300
Or they might go buy land.

196
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:07,500
Or it might be that they decide not to bother trading at all

197
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:09,400
because the costs are so high.

198
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:12,500
I remember when I was in high school,

199
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:16,400
in Los Angeles, theft of car CD players was rampant.

200
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:18,900
So if you had a CD player in your car,

201
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:20,800
the odds that your car would be broken into

202
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:25,200
and it would be stolen was probably about 50% per year.

203
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:29,200
And then one result was that people

204
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:31,600
would get extra alarm systems, things like that.

205
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:34,100
Another one is people had this weird kind of CD player

206
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:35,700
where every time you left your car,

207
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:38,500
you'd press a button and pop off the front of the CD player

208
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:42,600
and you would carry in your pocket 10% of the device

209
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:44,000
every time you left.

210
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,400
And the device was useless without it.

211
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:49,700
I guess a thief could go and steal and try

212
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:51,600
to pickpocket someone else for that piece

213
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:53,100
and then they would have two pieces.

214
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:54,600
But that would make it useless.

215
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:56,900
But probably the biggest fact is just a lot of people

216
00:08:56,900 --> 00:08:58,800
that would have liked to get a CD player just

217
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:01,500
didn't buy a CD player at all because of it.

218
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:03,100
So this is another deadly cost of it.

219
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:13,700
Now the point of this is not a new agey one of piracy

220
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:16,600
hurts you deep inside, man.

221
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:18,900
When you go and hurt another person, you hurt yourself.

222
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:20,900
We're all part of a circle of being.

223
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:22,900
One man is not an island.

224
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:24,500
So it's nothing like that.

225
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:27,800
That might all be true too.

226
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:32,300
But that is not the point of talking about deadly costs.

227
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:36,100
And it's not saying that piracy is bad for the pirates.

228
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:38,800
So ex-ante, the expectation is that pirates

229
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:40,900
are doing it because on average, it's better

230
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:43,100
for them than anything else.

231
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:45,900
The point is that pirates are gaining only a small fraction

232
00:09:45,900 --> 00:09:48,500
when the non-pirates are losing.

233
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:50,500
This does, again, make some assumptions

234
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:54,300
that ultimately require mind reading to be certain of,

235
00:09:54,300 --> 00:09:57,000
such as that the pirates don't enjoy the sight of gold

236
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,700
falling to the ocean floor.

237
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:03,100
That the pirates are not truly sadistic people,

238
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:05,100
just love watching other people die.

239
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:06,400
They're not misanthropes who like

240
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:08,400
seeing their fellow pirates die.

241
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:11,100
So there are always background assumptions

242
00:10:11,100 --> 00:10:13,900
that are being made, which normally are plausible.

243
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:18,700
Although, again, if you really go and scour,

244
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:19,700
not just the internet.

245
00:10:19,700 --> 00:10:22,500
This would also go to Wikipedia to see all the weird things

246
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:25,000
people have done, all of the serial killers that have

247
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,000
existed.

248
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,900
What was motivating those people?

249
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:31,300
You may not want to think about it too much late at night.

250
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:34,800
But it appears that there are some people who really

251
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:38,300
do enjoy torturing a human being that they have never met

252
00:10:38,300 --> 00:10:40,300
and has done them no harm to death.

253
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:42,500
It's not common, but they exist.

254
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:43,900
These are not made up people.

255
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:45,000
They're out there.

256
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,200
So you do.

257
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:50,400
Would people like that have been relatively inclined

258
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:52,400
to go into piracy backwards around?

